## 3. 🔌 FILE ACCESS & LARGE FILE ARCHITECTURE

### 3.1 Access Tiers (auto-selected)

| Tier | Mechanism | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| 1. SAF | ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT_TREE | Selected folder + subdirs (Play-safe) |
| 2. adb grant | MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE via adb shell appops | Full /sdcard as real java.io.File paths |
| 3. Root / Shizuku | libsu or Shizuku API | /data/, /system/, other app data dirs |

- Path sandbox: every model-returned path resolves and must stay inside the granted tree (unless "escalate outside workspace" is explicitly toggled)
- Binary guard: MIME detection before reading — refuse images/APKs/zips as text
- Zero-width file channel: read other apps' data dirs on supported devices without root

### 3.2 Large File Strategy (must support 100+ MB files)

Constraint: Gemini's 1M-token context cannot absorb 100 MB files (≈25M+ tokens). The app must never attempt a full-context load of large files. Files of any size are accessed via tiered read strategies.

| File size | Strategy |
|---|---|
| ≤ 200 KB | Full read into context |
| 200 KB – 5 MB | Line-range reads + grep; no full load unless explicitly requested |
| 5 MB – 100 MB | Local FTS5 index + targeted line-range reads + streaming hash for staleness |
| > 100 MB | Memory-mapped window reads only; never load whole file; pipe through external tools (ripgrep / strings / awk) via shell |

### 3.3 Large File Infrastructure
- Memory-mapped file access (MappedByteBuffer) for windowed reads — files > 100 MB read only the requested byte range
- Streaming SHA-256 for staleness detection — never load full file to compute hash
- Local SQLite FTS5 index stored in app-private storage, per-workspace, rebuildable
- Background indexing worker — index large folders while user chats
- Tool result cap of ~100 KB per call; larger results require pagination
- Token/cost HUD shows "large-file mode active" when a read is partial

### 3.4 Large File Behavior Per File Type
- Text files (code, smali, dump.cs, logs): line-range + grep only above 5 MB; index once, then search_index
- Binary files (.so, .dat, .obb, assets): never read as text; use read_bytes windows (max 1 MB per call) or hex view
- Archives (APK, ZIP, UnityFS, OBB): never load; zip_list builds a virtual tree, zip_read_entry reads individual entries
- libil2cpp.so (50–150 MB): read_bytes windows for ELF header/section analysis
- global-metadata.dat (20–100 MB): handled by Il2CppDumper; AI reads dump.cs output, not raw metadata
- dump.cs (50–200 MB): index_file → search_index; line-range reads for context
- Asset bundles (100 MB–2 GB): extracted entries only; never full-load

