## 13. 🎯 TARGET GAME PROFILES: CODM & MLBB

Both targets are Unity IL2CPP. The core pipeline (Il2CppDumper → offline analysis → Frida/memory patch) applies to both. The game profile layer tells the agent which anti-cheat posture it is operating under and adjusts tool selection accordingly.

### 13.1 CODM (Call of Duty Mobile)
- Engine: Unity IL2CPP
- Anti-cheat: Tencent ACE (kernel driver, process monitoring)
- Primary constraint: Offline metadata may be encrypted/protected → memory-dump-first workflow
- Tools:
  - CodMDumper / GameGuardian → memory-dump libil2cpp.so + global-metadata.dat
  - Il2CppDumper-for-COD → dedicated fork with Android relocations support
  - frida-il2cpp-bridge → runtime dump if memory dump is blocked
- Risk level: HIGH. Kernel-level detection. Test accounts only for runtime hooks.

### 13.2 MLBB (Mobile Legends: Bang Bang)
- Engine: Unity IL2CPP
- Anti-cheat: Moonton in-house (active ban waves, 30-year bans)
- Primary constraint: Runtime memory manipulation is heavily monitored → asset-layer patching is the dominant low-risk path
- Tools:
  - libtool2.0 → Shizuku-based UnityFS v7 patcher + native IL2CPP reflection (no root)
  - mlbb-esp-ksu pattern → external /proc/pid/mem reader + SurfaceFlinger overlay (no code injection)
  - Standard Il2CppDumper workflow for offset extraction
- Risk level: MEDIUM-HIGH. Asset patches on test accounts preferred; runtime hooks carry ban risk.

### 13.3 Agent Behavior When a Game Profile Is Detected
When the user selects a workspace containing com.activision.callofduty.shooter or com.mobile.legends, the app auto-suggests the matching profile:
- CODM → default to memory-dump toolchain, warn before any Frida attach
- MLBB → default to asset-inspection toolchain (UnityFS listing/parsing), offer external-read pattern before suggesting injection
- Both → surface a banner: "This target has kernel/active anti-cheat. Runtime operations should be treated as test-account-only."

